WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.031 --> 00:00:02.615
Loops are a fundamental concept in

2
00:00:02.735 --> 00:00:03.456
programming.

3
00:00:04.097 --> 00:00:06.641
They help us repeat actions in our code.

4
00:00:07.382 --> 00:00:10.006
This means we can avoid writing the same

5
00:00:10.127 --> 00:00:11.929
code over and over again.

6
00:00:11.969 --> 00:00:15.475
Imagine having to print numbers from one

7
00:00:15.555 --> 00:00:16.036
to ten.

8
00:00:16.657 --> 00:00:18.600
Without loops, you'd have to write ten

9
00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:20.082
print statements!

10
00:00:20.082 --> 00:00:22.150
But with loops, we can do it in

11
00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:23.294
just a few lines.

12
00:00:23.876 --> 00:00:26.346
There are two main types of loops: for

13
00:00:26.426 --> 00:00:27.771
loops and while loops.

14
00:00:28.594 --> 00:00:31.405
Each has its own strengths and uses.

15
00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:34.213
Now, let’s dive into for loops.

16
00:00:34.934 --> 00:00:37.256
For loops are great because they iterate

17
00:00:37.417 --> 00:00:40.140
over a sequence, like a list.

18
00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:42.383
They execute a block of code for each

19
00:00:42.583 --> 00:00:43.765
item in that sequence.

20
00:00:44.425 --> 00:00:46.808
This makes them perfect for tasks that

21
00:00:46.868 --> 00:00:49.371
require repetition over collections.

22
00:00:50.132 --> 00:00:52.636
For example, if we want to print numbers

23
00:00:52.696 --> 00:00:55.119
from zero to four, we can use a

24
00:00:55.199 --> 00:00:57.021
for loop like this.

25
00:00:57.021 --> 00:00:59.644
For number in range five, we print the

26
00:00:59.704 --> 00:01:00.024
number.

27
00:01:00.665 --> 00:01:02.487
Let’s take a look at that in action.

28
00:01:03.788 --> 00:01:06.350
Next up, we have while loops.

29
00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:08.893
While loops repeat as long as a condition

30
00:01:09.033 --> 00:01:09.514
is true.

31
00:01:09.574 --> 00:01:12.677
This is really useful when we don’t know

32
00:01:12.717 --> 00:01:14.819
how many times we need to repeat

33
00:01:14.899 --> 00:01:15.299
something.

34
00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:18.382
For instance, we can use a while loop

35
00:01:18.443 --> 00:01:20.164
to count from zero to four.

36
00:01:20.865 --> 00:01:22.907
But we need to be careful!

37
00:01:22.907 --> 00:01:25.655
If we forget to update our condition, we

38
00:01:25.736 --> 00:01:27.681
might end up with an infinite loop.

39
00:01:28.424 --> 00:01:30.750
So always ensure your loop has a way

40
00:01:30.791 --> 00:01:31.352
to stop.

41
00:01:31.954 --> 00:01:33.960
Here’s how that looks in code.

42
00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:36.923
So, when should you use each type of

43
00:01:36.963 --> 00:01:37.324
loop?

44
00:01:37.965 --> 00:01:39.747
For loops are best when you know the

45
00:01:39.807 --> 00:01:41.750
number of iterations in advance.

46
00:01:42.551 --> 00:01:44.514
They’re straightforward and easy to

47
00:01:44.594 --> 00:01:44.895
read.

48
00:01:45.596 --> 00:01:48.219
On the other hand, while loops shine when

49
00:01:48.259 --> 00:01:50.262
you need to repeat something based on a

50
00:01:50.322 --> 00:01:50.903
condition.

51
00:01:51.564 --> 00:01:53.487
Choosing the right loop can really

52
00:01:53.607 --> 00:01:55.890
simplify your code and make it more

53
00:01:55.951 --> 00:01:56.491
efficient.

54
00:01:57.292 --> 00:01:59.796
Let’s visualize these concepts.

55
00:02:00.383 --> 00:02:02.105
Now it’s time to practice!

56
